WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
It's a new day on SpaceX Daily, episode 41, coming to you from Vancouver.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:13.360
It's July 22nd, 2026. Plenty moving across Starship, Falcon, and Starlink today.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:19.620
Northrop Grumman's MRV-1 servicer is now on orbit after its Falcon 9 ride,

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:25.000
beginning the first on-orbit installation of propulsion pods that could extend the life

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:31.560
of three aging commercial satellites. SpaceX has slipped Starship Flight 13 by one day,

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:38.320
now targeting a July 23 lift-off from Starbase. The vehicle completed its pre-flight checks

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:44.440
after an earlier scrub, keeping the test objectives for booster catch and ship reentry intact.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:51.280
This continues the flight test campaign that last flew in June, with observers tracking whether

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:57.360
the new date allows the full set of orbital refueling demonstrations to stay on the original timeline.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:03.020
The slip follows the pattern of prior test flights where last-minute data reviews have

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:09.640
protected the core hardware objectives without altering the overall campaign arc. While Starship pushes

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:16.700
the frontier of heavy lift reusability, today's Falcon 9 flight shows the same reuse logic already

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:23.440
operating at scale on a different mission. A Falcon 9 lifted off from Space Launch Complex 40

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:31.020
at Cape Canaveral, carrying Northrop Grumman's MRV-1 robotic servicer. The previously flown booster

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:35.700
performed a nominal ascent and returned for a landing at the designated Cape Zone.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:42.020
The mission will demonstrate installation of propulsion pods on three geostationary satellites

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:48.120
to extend their operational life using robotic arms and a docking system developed under a DARPA

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:55.700
program. The Falcon 9 placed the MRV-1 into a super-synchronous transfer orbit that will take it to

00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:02.240
geostationary altitude while minimizing the servicer's own propellant use. Ground teams confirmed

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:08.000
initial acquisition of signals shortly after separation and are now performing early checkout

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:14.720
of the robotic systems before the long transfer to GEO. This marks the first commercial attempt

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:20.740
at on-orbit satellite life extension using external hardware, with data from the first three

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:25.760
installations expected to inform whether the approach scales to additional operators.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:31.660
Photographers captured the Falcon 9 ascent and booster landing at landing zone 1.

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:37.220
The images show the payload-faring separation sequence and the first stage return burn.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:43.760
Public release of these photos provides the first visual record of the MRV-1 stack configuration.

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:49.820
The sequence documents the faring halves separating cleanly at the planned altitude.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:55.620
Live coverage confirmed nominal performance through max-q and stage separation.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:01.540
The booster returned to the Cape landing zone while the upper stage continued to the target orbit.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:06.900
Early telemetry indicated the MRV-1 had begun its commissioning sequence.

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:12.600
The payload-faring protected the MRV-1 during ascent through the dense atmosphere.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:18.760
Post-separation, the servicer deployed its solar arrays and began attitude control checks.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:24.740
Early ground station passes verified that the arrays had locked onto the sunlight as planned.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:30.560
The mission demonstrates how existing launch vehicles can support a new class of in-space

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:37.520
infrastructure. The second stage performed a single burn to place MRV-1 on its correct trajectory.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:43.160
Telemetry showed nominal performance through the coast phase prior to the final insertion

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:49.320
burn. The same first principles logic that makes this servicing approach attractive is unpacked

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:56.100
in today's engineering deep dive. Observers on X noted the clean separation of the MRV-1

00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:00.000
from the Falcon 9 second stage and the prompt array deployment.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:05.520
Several accounts highlighted the mission's use of a previously flown booster as evidence

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:11.900
of routine reuse operations, while independent trackers posted preliminary orbital elements

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:16.200
showing the servicer on a standard geostationary transfer trajectory.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:22.200
One analyst pointed out that the DARPA heritage of the robotic system could accelerate future

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:26.380
commercial servicing contracts if the first three pod installations succeed.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:32.700
From an engineering standpoint, the MRV-1 servicer introduces a new operational mode

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:38.100
for geostationary satellites by shifting the mass budget away from propellant carried at launch.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:44.660
The dominant cost driver for GEO assets has been the mass of propellant carried at launch

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:48.940
and the limited specific impulse of onboard chemical systems.

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:55.020
By delivering a pod with electric thrusters after the satellite has already reached orbit,

00:04:55.500 --> 00:05:00.880
the mission moves the heavy lifting to on-orbit servicing hardware instead of the launch pad.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:06.960
This reduces the idiot index of the overall system because the expensive launch mass is now

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:12.520
spent only once on the core satellite, while the life extension hardware rides a separate

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:18.340
lower cost trajectory. The robotic docking and attachment sequence must solve the same

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:24.240
relative motion and contact dynamics problems that starship will face during orbital refueling,

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:30.420
yet at a smaller scale and with lower energy. Success here would demonstrate that the same

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:36.360
first principles approach of moving heavy lifting to orbit can be applied to existing satellite

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:42.480
fleets. Next data points to watch are the actual propellant savings measured on the first three

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:47.800
target satellites and the repeatability of the pod attachment mechanism under thermal and

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:54.500
radiation conditions at GEO. The electric thruster pods also change the station-keeping

00:05:54.500 --> 00:06:00.520
equation by trading chemical delta V for higher efficiency electric propulsion once attached,

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:05.620
which directly extends the revenue generating lifetime of each host satellite,

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:13.240
without requiring a new bus design. On the AI front, SpaceX will supply engineering data to train

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:19.600
GROC's next model while avoiding any restricted material. The arrangement gives GROC access to

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:25.800
proprietary hardware and operations data that competing AI labs cannot replicate, covering

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:31.480
vehicle performance metrics and manufacturing parameters without crossing export control boundaries.

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:38.600
SpaceX is positioned as a potential multi-billion dollar customer for AI services to the Pentagon

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:44.560
through its existing satellite and communications infrastructure, with Starlink and Starsheald networks

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:50.640
potentially serving as the data backbone for defense AI workloads focused on low latency routing

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:56.640
of sensor data. The data flows that will refine these servicing techniques are also feeding another

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:03.460
part of the Musk ecosystem. Worth keeping an eye on, Ray Dalio stated he had advised Elon Musk

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:09.580
against concentrating his personal capital in SpaceX and Mars-related efforts. The warning

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:16.480
centers on the long timeline and technical risk of achieving routine Mars access. Dalio's comments

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:22.520
come as SpaceX continues to fund both near-term launch operations and the Starship Development

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:28.660
Program from the same corporate resources. Resolution would require either demonstrated progress

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:35.200
on Mars' architecture milestones, or clearer separation of investor and founder capital allocation.

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:46.340
And a quick market note, SPC-X closed at $123.54, down 1.2% from the prior close, with the first

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:52.340
earnings call date now announced. Before we go, the next data point to watch is whether the July

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:59.800
23 Starship window holds and delivers the first on-orbit refueling demo. That's the day at SpaceX.

